  "queue_policy_note": "No GPU work runs outside the queue. The 'unqueued' condition is a burner started INSIDE this ts job with its own session and no JOB_ID, so it models a tenant invisible to the queue channel. That is the detection question; it is not a decision to bypass the queue.",
  "discard_rule": {
    "proposed": [
      "DROP the sample if power.draw at either boundary exceeds 150 W, or utilization.gpu exceeds 50 %, or temperature.gpu exceeds 50 C. Rationale from this run: uncontended reads 25.9/77 W, 6 %, 38-40 C; BOTH contended conditions read 574 W, 97-98 %, 57-60 C. The gap is an order of magnitude on power, so the threshold is not delicate.",
      "DROP the sample if clocks.sm at a boundary is below 0.75x the max SM clock seen in the same run (the idle floor here read 195-300 MHz against 2490-2760 MHz under load).",
      "RETAIN the queue-state record as the PRIMARY channel for contention routed through the queue, and telemetry as the primary channel for contention that is not. Neither alone is sufficient: in this run queue_state reported n_other_active=0 and n_other_running=0 in ALL THREE conditions, including the two with a burner resident on the device, because the burner is a child of this job and has no JOB_ID of its own."
    ],
    "do_not_rely_on": "clocks_throttle_reasons.active as the PRIMARY signal. It did go non-zero here (0x4 = SW power cap) because the burner I chose sits at the 575 W limit, but the reviewer's own unqueued sweeps ran with it at 0x0 throughout. A detector built on it would have caught this run and missed theirs.",
    "residual_risk": "Light contention that does not move power, utilisation, temperature or clocks is undetectable by ANY channel available here, and neither is queue_state. For it the only defences are the estimator itself -- the three-invocation median plus the max_cv gate -- and process discipline. This is the uncomfortable claim the bead exists to surface, so it is stated rather than smoothed over."
  },
  "process_discipline": {
    "queue_slot": "the whole experiment ran inside ONE ts job",
    "burner_placement": "started INSIDE that job; no GPU work ran outside the queue. Condition C models a tenant invisible to the queue channel, which is the detection question, not a decision to bypass the queue",
    "no_clock_locking": "no -lgc, no sudo, no persistence-mode change; gpu-sim-u2o.6.4 stays held",
    "untrusted_kernels": "none: the cells are 6.6.2's hand-written anchor kernels"
  }
